 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Sampling Performance:** Sampling a single multi-channel texture is faster than sampling multiple independent textures due to better cache locality [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Overhead:** Reduces memory overhead by eliminating redundant texture headers and allowing tighter packing [S2, unofficial]. However, if source textures have vastly different resolutions, the unified texture may waste space on lower-resolution channels [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Limited to 4 channels per texture [S1, unofficial]. Linear filtering across resolution boundaries can cause blurring if not managed [S1, unofficial]. Precision loss occurs with 8-bit channels [S3]. 2-channel textures are notably slower on many GPUs [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Packing is $O(N \cdot D_u^3)$ where $N$ is the number of source textures and $D_u$ is the unified dimension. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel, retrieving 4 values instead of 1, reducing memory bandwidth for multi-attribute access [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Reduces memory overhead by eliminating redundant texture headers and allowing tighter packing. However, if source textures have vastly different resolutions, the unified texture may waste space on lower-resolution channels [S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Improves performance when multiple attributes are accessed per voxel (e.g., density + noise + light) by reducing texture unit switches and sampling calls [S1, unofficial]. Texture sampling a single multi-channel texture is faster than sampling multiple independent textures due to better cache locality [S2, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** Linearly scales with unified volume resolution. GPU performance degrades if 2-channel formats are used; padding to 3 channels or splitting into 1-channel textures restores performance [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths and Limitations:** Best when attributes are tightly coupled and accessed simultaneously per voxel. Limited to 4 channels per texture, and 8-bit formats introduce quantization [S3]. Linear filtering across resolution boundaries may blur high-frequency details [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Use when rendering volumes with multiple coincident attributes (noise, masks, densities) and cache locality is critical [S2, unofficial]. Avoid when attributes require different storage precisions, resolutions, or exceed 4 channels [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Used in custom volume applications (e.g., gloo) for fluorescence data and voxel-to-object ID maps [S2, unofficial]. Popularized in Unity-based volumetric tutorials for efficient noise generation and sampling, where techniques like horizontal packing of 128x128x128 3D textures into 16384x128 2D images are employed [S3]. Also applied in PBR material pipelines for packing material properties [S4, unofficial].
+Used in volumetric rendering for smoke and clouds, where multiple noise functions (Perlin, Worley) and masks are required per voxel [S1, unofficial][S3]. Also used in custom volume apps (e.g., gloo-based fluorescence data with float16, and voxel-to-object ID maps with uint8 RGB) [S2, unofficial]. Applied in PBR material pipelines for packing material properties [S4, unofficial].
 
